WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.027 --> 00:00:04.728
- [Narrator] This is the
Attwater's prairie chicken.

00:00:04.728 --> 00:00:07.597
(gentle piano music)

00:00:07.597 --> 00:00:11.429
A century ago, there were a
million of them roaming around.

00:00:11.429 --> 00:00:14.648
Today, less than 100 are left in the wild,

00:00:14.648 --> 00:00:17.246
and are considered one of
the most endangered birds

00:00:17.246 --> 00:00:18.663
in North America.

00:00:20.578 --> 00:00:25.189
They are native to the coastal
areas of Texas and Louisiana.

00:00:25.189 --> 00:00:27.288
Attwater's prairie chickens are small,

00:00:27.288 --> 00:00:29.989
averaging about a
foot-and-a-half in a size,

00:00:29.989 --> 00:00:32.509
and weighing two pounds.

00:00:32.509 --> 00:00:35.619
They prefer short and tall
grass in their habitats,

00:00:35.619 --> 00:00:38.668
and have a diverse diet,
including grass shoots,

00:00:38.668 --> 00:00:41.001
flowers, seeds and insects.

00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:44.589
Urbanization and other
forms of habitat loss

00:00:44.589 --> 00:00:46.419
have been one of the major factors

00:00:46.419 --> 00:00:49.819
in the steep decline of their population.

00:00:49.819 --> 00:00:52.806
The Houston Zoo runs one of
the captive breeding programs

00:00:52.806 --> 00:00:55.858
to help reintroduce these
critically endangered birds

00:00:55.858 --> 00:00:57.597
to the wild.

00:00:57.597 --> 00:01:00.847
This is the Attwater's prairie chicken.

